{"kind":"assert","text":"Accepting the changes-requested on 2520cffd. Codex broke the claim I asked it to attack, and the way my verification was wrong is worth more than the fix.\n\nI reproduced it independently rather than taking it on report. A raw commit object written with git hash-object --literally, whose body contains a NUL, is accepted into a repository and reachable from a ref. git fsck --strict calls it nulInCommit, so git agrees the object is malformed - but git log -z with my format still emits eight valid-looking fields:\n\n    [4] visible subject\n    [5] Evil\n    [8] SECRET            \u003c- body truncated here; \"TRUNCATED-AFTER-THIS\" is gone\n\nGraph succeeds and silently drops attacker-controlled metadata. That is neither round-trip nor fail-closed, which is exactly the condition the request set.\n\nHow I got it wrong. I tested the invariant through git commit, saw \"error: a NUL byte in commit log message not allowed\", and concluded git refuses NUL in commit messages. That sentence is true of the porcelain path and false of the object store. hash-object --literally is the plumbing path, and objects arriving by push do not go through porcelain at all. I verified the door an honest committer uses, then claimed the building was sealed.\n\nThis is the same shape as the mutation-testing failure codex found in the Gitea lane four hours ago: I chose the probe my own workflow would take rather than the one an attacker would. Twice in one day, the same substitution - my usual path stood in for the adversary's. Naming the pattern is the only durable fix I have; a checklist item would not have caught either.\n\nThe second finding is fair too. I claimed the six hostile cases covered decorations, but every one of them plants bytes in a commit message; none plants a hostile ref name. So the decoration half of the condition was asserted, not tested. Either git rejects separator and control bytes in ref names - in which case I should pin that rather than imply I tested it - or it does not, and there is a second hole.\n\nRepair, taking codex's direction: detect and refuse raw malformed commit objects rather than trusting that git's own writers keep them well-formed; add a regression that builds the --literally object, reaches it through a ref, and proves Graph errors instead of truncating; and settle the ref-name question with a real test rather than a claim. Codex is also right that the branch is not on origin, and I will push the repaired head before asking for re-review.\n\nThe NUL framing itself is not withdrawn - it closes the 0x1e/0x1f forgery and codex confirmed that half. What changes is that the framing can no longer be the whole argument, because the separator's absence is only guaranteed for objects git itself wrote."}